KNOWNBUG
main.cpp
--cpp11
^\[main\.assertion\.1\] line \d+ function template: SUCCESS$
^\[main\.assertion\.2\] line \d+ member of a class template: SUCCESS$
^\[main\.assertion\.3\] line \d+ WRONG \(must FAIL\): FAILURE$
^VERIFICATION FAILED$
^EXIT=10$
^SIGNAL=0$
--
^warning: ignoring
^CONVERSION ERROR$
--
N5008 [expr.prim.lambda.closure]/1, [temp.spec.general]/1: the lambda in
size_of<C> has a distinct closure type, with its own call operator, in
size_of<char> and size_of<long>.  The front end creates the closure once,
for the first specialization, and uses it in every later one: both calls
return sizeof(char) (bin53 too).  In libstdc++'s <format>
(cpp11_libstdcxx_header_drops/test_format) the wchar_t specialization of
_Formatting_scanner::_M_format_arg uses the char specialization's visitor
closure, and the bodies dropped there are its operator()<int> and
basic_format_arg<wchar_t context>::_M_visit; that this is the only cause
is not yet confirmed.  g++ 13 and clang++
18 accept this program, and it fails only its WRONG assertion.
